The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
memcpy(&um->id.daddr, &x->id.daddr, sizeof(um->id.daddr));
um->id.spi = x->id.spi;
um->id.family = x->props.family;
um->id.proto = x->id.proto;
```
Reason:
The struct `xfrm_usersa_id` contains a one-byte padding hole after the `proto` field. Without zeroing the entire structure before assignment, this padding remains uninitialized. When the message is copied to userspace via netlink, the uninitialized padding leaks kernel memory information.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
memset(&um->id, 0, sizeof(um->id));
```
Reason:
Adding `memset` initializes the entire `um->id` structure to zero, including the padding hole. This ensures no uninitialized data is copied to userspace, preventing the information leak.

